<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  demo_robot — differential-drive robot for the Aquila AM69 ROS 2 demo.

  Runs on: x86 host only (Gazebo/RViz2 never run on the module — CLAUDE.md rule 1).

  ============================ WHY THIS IS A WRAPPER ============================

  This file used to hand-assemble the robot from individual TurtleBot 4 meshes
  (shell.dae, tower_standoff.dae, rplidar.dae, ...) with offsets derived by
  measuring mesh bounding boxes. That approach was abandoned: every mesh carries
  its own internal origin and rotation, so re-parenting the parts onto our own
  link tree scattered them visibly, and each re-measurement fixed one part while
  moving another.

  We now include the upstream, fully-assembled model:

      /opt/ros/jazzy/share/nav2_minimal_tb4_description/urdf/standard/turtlebot4.urdf.xacro

  It is maintained by the Nav2 team, its offsets are correct by construction, and
  it ships the DiffDrive + JointStatePublisher system plugins plus a richer sensor
  set (RPLIDAR A1, OAK-D RGBD stereo camera, IMU) than we had.

  Do NOT re-add per-part meshes here. If the robot looks wrong, fix it upstream
  or override a joint below — do not rebuild the assembly.

  ========================== WHAT THIS WRAPPER CHANGES ==========================

  Upstream is a TurtleBot 4, not "our" robot, so three things need overriding to
  satisfy this project's invariants. Each is done by re-declaring the element
  AFTER the include — xacro's last-definition-wins applies to <gazebo> blocks.

  1. gz topic names. Upstream's DiffDrive uses /cmd_vel + /odom and its sensors
     use /scan and /rgbd_camera/*. Those are already short/unscoped names, which
     is what demo_simulation/config/bridge_warehouse.yaml needs, so they are kept
     as-is. See that file for the mapping to the /demo/* contract.

  2. Odometry frame. Upstream's DiffDrive publishes odom -> base_link, and its
     child_frame_id is HARDCODED in
     nav2_minimal_tb4_description/urdf/icreate/create3.urdf.xacro with no xacro
     arg to override it.

     Re-declaring the plugin here does NOT override it. xacro does not merge or
     replace <gazebo> blocks — it concatenates them, so the expanded URDF ends up
     with TWO DiffDrive plugins driving the same two joints. That was tried and
     rejected. Count the plugin ELEMENTS, not text matches — any grep also hits
     this very comment. test_exactly_one_of_each_gz_system_plugin does it properly.

     So the frame is reconciled on the Nav2 side instead: robot_base_frame is
     base_link in demo_navigation/config/nav2_params.yaml.

     This is safe, not a compromise. The TF tree direction differs from our old
     model:
         ours (before): base_footprint (root) -> base_link
         upstream:      base_link (root)      -> base_footprint
     and base_footprint_joint is a fixed IDENTITY transform (xyz 0 0 0, rpy 0 0 0),
     so odom -> base_link and odom -> base_footprint are numerically the same
     edge. Nav2 gains nothing from base_footprint here and both frames sit at the
     same height, because upstream places base_link at ground level rather than at
     wheel-axle height like our old model did.

  3. Velocity limits. Upstream clamps to 0.5 m/s and 2.0 rad/s inside the plugin.
     Nav2's controller limits must not exceed these or the controller commands a
     speed the simulator silently refuses to reach, which reads as a tuning
     problem. They are matched in nav2_params.yaml.

  Override at launch time, e.g.:
      xacro demo_robot.urdf.xacro robot_name:=demo_robot
-->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro" name="demo_robot">

  <!-- Identity. Must match the -name passed to `ros_gz_sim create`. -->
  <xacro:arg name="robot_name" default="demo_robot"/>

  <!--
    Accepted and intentionally ignored.

    The old hand-built model had a use_meshes:=false path that fell back to
    primitives for renderer-less arm64 images. Upstream is mesh-only, so there is
    nothing to switch. This arg is kept ONLY so that existing launch files and
    documented commands that pass use_meshes:=... keep working instead of dying
    on an "unknown argument" error.

    Nothing about the module side depends on it: no OGRE 2 application (Gazebo,
    RViz2) ever runs on the Aquila, so the module never renders this model at all.
  -->
  <xacro:arg name="use_meshes" default="true"/>

  <!--
    The upstream model. Absolute-path include via $(find ...) so it resolves
    through the ament share tree, not a relative path out of this package.
  -->
  <xacro:include filename="$(find nav2_minimal_tb4_description)/urdf/standard/turtlebot4.urdf.xacro"/>

  <!--
    ############################################################################
    #  REQUIRED POST-PROCESSING — THE ROBOT FALLS APART WITHOUT IT             #
    ############################################################################

    Upstream tags 21 of its fixed joints with

        <gazebo reference="..."><preserveFixedJoint>true</preserveFixedJoint></gazebo>

    which tells Gazebo NOT to weld the joint's child into its parent's rigid body.
    Spawned as-is, this robot is 13 INDEPENDENT PHYSICS BODIES (shell, four tower
    standoffs, sensor plate, lidar, camera, bumper, wheels, caster, base) held
    together only by fixed-joint constraints. The solver does not hold them
    rigidly: under gravity and contact the parts settle and drift, the tower leans,
    and the plate and lidar float off the standoffs.

    That is the long-standing "robot with expanded/scattered parts" symptom, and it
    is NOT a geometry bug — the offsets and per-visual rpy rotations here are
    correct. Welding changes nothing about where parts are DRAWN, only whether
    physics may move them relative to each other. Re-measuring mesh offsets and
    swapping the robot model both failed to fix it for exactly this reason.

    xacro CANNOT remove a tag that an include emits, so the strip happens in the
    pipeline that feeds Gazebo:

        xacro demo_robot.urdf.xacro | scripts/weld_fixed_joints.py > robot.urdf

    demo_simulation/launch/simulation.launch.py does this. If you spawn this xacro
    by any other route, pipe it through that script too, or the robot comes apart.

    RViz2 is unaffected — it ignores <gazebo> blocks entirely and always draws the
    assembled robot. So the model looks perfect in RViz and falls apart in Gazebo,
    which is what makes this hard to attribute.
  -->

  <!--
    ===================== Inherited from upstream, unchanged =====================

    Deliberately NOT re-declared here (see note 2 in the file header — xacro
    concatenates <gazebo> blocks instead of overriding them, so re-declaring any
    of these produces a duplicate plugin rather than a replacement):

      DiffDrive              gz topics /cmd_vel, /odom, tf; odom -> base_link;
                             wheel_separation 0.233, wheel_radius 0.03575;
                             clamps 0.5 m/s and 2.0 rad/s
      JointStatePublisher    /joint_states for left_wheel_joint, right_wheel_joint
      RPLIDAR A1             gz /scan, 360 samples, 10 Hz, frame rplidar_link
      OAK-D RGBD             gz /rgbd_camera/{image,depth_image,points,camera_info}
      IMU                    gz /imu at 200 Hz

    wheel_separation and wheel_radius are the two values that must never drift
    from the physical model: the plugin integrates them into odometry, so a
    mismatch produces a robot that slides while reporting a straight line, with no
    error anywhere. Inheriting them keeps them locked to the meshes by definition.

    The IMU only publishes if the WORLD loads gz-sim-imu-system. The bundled
    nav2_minimal_tb4_sim warehouse.sdf does; a hand-written world may not, and the
    sensor then stays silent with no error. Verify with `gz topic -l | grep imu`.

    All gz topic names above are literal/unscoped, which is what
    demo_simulation/config/bridge_warehouse.yaml binds to. Gazebo also advertises
    /model/<name>/* lookalikes with no publisher behind them; bridging to those
    yields a robot that never moves, silently. See the long note in that file.
  -->

</robot>
